\pard\sa180\fs22 THE STUDIO  /  ISSUE 01\par
\pard\sa260\b\fs60 Notes on a better editor\b0\par
\pard\sa320\fs38 Some ideas need a sentence. Others need a little more room.\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs40 Start with the words\b0\par
\pard\sa260\sl280\slmult1\fs34 A good editor lets you focus on what you want to say. Choose a typeface, give a heading some weight, and leave a little space for the next thought.\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs40 Make the details count\b0\par
\pard\sa220\fs34 Style a sentence, add a link, or turn a few lines into a list. This is a working document: \highlight1\cf2 select a passage and make it yours\highlight0\cf0 .\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs34 01. Shape the page\b0\line Use the formatting tools for type, color, alignment, and lists.\par
\pard\sa180\b 02. Follow the text\b0\line Track a selection, then edit around it. Your highlight follows along.\par
\pard\sa280\b 03. Add something useful\b0\line Place an inline widget or an action beside a line. The words stay intact.\par
\pard\li240\ri240\sa280\i\fs36 The document keeps your words. The view makes room for your tools.\i0\par
\pard\sa160\b\fs40 Your next draft\b0\par
\pard\sa220\fs34 Replace this page with your own notes, or choose New for a blank document. The RTF reference is still available in the formatting toolbar.\par
